  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nAnother day with fog as the only sensible weather concern. Latest \nsatellite and surface obs show dense fog rapidly expanding across \nour eastern CWA thanks to another night of light easterly flow off \nLake Erie and prime radiational cooling. High clouds that were in \nplace earlier thanks to upstream trough have now largely dissipated \nfor most of the area and see no reason why fog won't continue to \nexpand and persist through the late morning as suggested by plethora \nof latest guidance. Only question is how far west and south it will \nbuild. With light E to even ESE flow, confidence is not high for our \nsouthwest zones. Latest satellite shows a rapid expansion to the NW \nbut very little southward expansion. Issued a dense fog advisory but \nleft off the far west and southern counties for now. Will continue \nto monitor obs and expand as necessary. Surface winds finally veer \nto the south tonight and more high clouds should limit fog potential \n(save perhaps for our extreme NE areas where Lake Erie could still \nbe a factor).\n\nHighs today a near carbon copy from yesterday given no thermal \nadvection. Highs do creep up a few degrees Sun and Mon as very light \nsoutherly flow/minor WAA takes shape. A weak backdoor front then \nbrings a brief return to cool easterly flow for Tue but WAA \nincreases again for Wed and Thu as a decaying cold front approaches \nthe region. This front looks to stall and wash out just to our NW \nwith just some very low chances for light rain near Lake MI Thu into \nFri. Better rain chances arrive over the weekend but there is still \na high degree of spread regarding exact timing and amounts.",
